Hermes Autonomous Crypto Trading System — v2 (Edge-Gated Architecture)
Revision of the September 2026 architecture blueprint. This version inserts an explicit, non-bypassable edge-validation gate ahead of the strategy and execution layers, resolves what "Hermes" actually is, and marks the strategies already falsified by prior testing so they cannot silently re-enter production.

0. What Changed From v1, and Why
v1 was a strong execution/safety architecture with one structural gap: it treated the Strategy Proposal Layer (Momentum, Trend Following, Breakout, Mean Reversion, VWAP Reversion, Order Flow) as ready-to-build components, without reference to the fact that this exact strategy family had already been tested on real BTC/USDT data and returned a decisive NO CREDIBLE EDGE verdict — twice (Phase 0 and Phase 0b), across six independent pre-registered experiments, with bootstrap confidence intervals entirely negative and two promising-looking filters failing out-of-sample replication.

v2 makes three changes:

An Edge Validation Gate is now a first-class, blocking phase. No strategy — new or already-falsified — reaches the Strategy Proposal Layer without a fresh, pre-registered, out-of-sample pass. Falsified strategies are recorded permanently and cannot be silently reintroduced.
"Hermes" is now explicitly defined as a deterministic/statistical decision layer, not an LLM reasoning agent, closing the ambiguity in v1 between "avoid asking an LLM to interpret raw events" (Section 6) and a confidence / free-text decision_rationale field that implied LLM output (Section 9–10).
The roadmap is reordered so infrastructure investment (n8n execution, order lifecycle, learning pipeline, strategy versioning) is explicitly gated behind having at least one strategy that survives the same bar Phase 0/0b already applied — rather than being built in parallel with an unresolved research question.
Everything else from v1 — the risk engine, the audit trail, the failure-handling table, the traceability requirement — was already sound and is carried forward with only clarifying edits.

1. Executive Summary
This document defines the target architecture for an autonomous crypto trading platform in which Hermes continuously observes real-time market conditions, builds structured market context, generates and evaluates trading proposals that have already cleared a pre-registered edge-validation gate, makes BUY/SELL/HOLD decisions, passes only deterministic risk-approved actions to an n8n execution workflow, monitors the resulting orders and positions, analyzes completed trades, records structured lessons, validates strategy improvements, and feeds approved strategy versions back into Hermes.

The existing market-data platform is treated as a completed foundational layer. Hermes consumes its API and WebSocket interfaces rather than duplicating market-data collection. Binance remains downstream of n8n for execution. The architecture separates probabilistic analysis from deterministic safety controls and prevents a single losing trade from directly changing production strategy. It also prevents a strategy without a validated edge from ever reaching the risk engine in the first place.

